[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Graph Theory and Traversal Strategies in Problem Solving"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Graphs are foundational data structures used to model pairwise relationships between objects. Mastering graph theory and its traversal strategies is essential for solving complex routing, networking, and dependency problems in computer science."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of Graph Theory"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A graph G = (V, E) consists of a set of vertices V (nodes) and a set of edges E (connections). Graphs can be directed or undirected, weighted or unweighted. Understanding how to represent these structures in code is the first step toward algorithmic problem solving."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adjacency Matrix: A 2D array where cell (i, j) indicates the presence and weight of an edge between vertex i and vertex j."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adjacency List: An array or hash map of lists where each vertex stores a collection of its adjacent vertices. This is memory efficient for sparse graphs."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Depth-First Search (DFS)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Depth-First Search explores as deep as possible along each branch before backtracking. It is typically implemented using recursion or an explicit stack. DFS is ideal for topological sorting, cycle detection, and solving maze-like puzzles."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"function dfs(node, visited, adjList) {
  visited.add(node);
  console.log(node);
  for (let neighbor of adjList[node]) {
    if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
      dfs(neighbor, visited, adjList);
    }
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Breadth-First Search (BFS)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Breadth-First Search explores the graph level by level, visiting all neighbors of a node before moving to the next level. Implemented using a queue, BFS is guaranteed to find the shortest path in an unweighted graph."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"function bfs(startNode, adjList) {
  let visited = new Set();
  let queue = [startNode];
  visited.add(startNode);

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    let currentNode = queue.shift();
    console.log(currentNode);

    for (let neighbor of adjList[currentNode]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor);
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing between DFS and BFS depends entirely on the problem requirements. Use DFS when you need to explore all paths or analyze tree structures deeply. Use BFS when finding the shortest path or minimum steps in an unweighted graph is required."}]
